        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Exposure to certain toxic compounds can cause serious damage to body tissues. The eyes are
particularly vulnerable. A variety of sequelae can result from a toxic eye exposure, ranging from
irritation to blindness. The standard first aid treatment is to quickly flush the eyes with copious
amounts of water using an eyewash.
<p>
The classic terrestrial eyewash is considered a standard for laboratory and industrial facilities
where exposure to toxic and irritant chemicals is a distinct possibility. It is typically a small sink that, when activated, directs two streams of water directly into the eyes. An eyewash can deliver copious quantities of water for time periods ranging from 20 minutes to several hours. The
duration of eye washing is based on the extent of the toxic chemical exposure and the specific
irritant properties of that compound.
<p>
The Space Shuttle is frequently utilized as a laboratory facility with a variety of on-going
experiments. There are also numerous compounds used in the propulsion, cooling, auxiliary
power, and environmental control systems that are toxic. An eye exposure, during a mission, to
any of these potentially toxic compounds is not an impossibility. The Space Station Freedom
shares the same concerns about toxic contamination exposure. There is presently no adequate method of flushing the eyes with water in the microgravity. The current Contingency Eye Wash
Procedure proposes that the crew flush the eyes with either a syringe and potable water or an IV bag with 500cc of normal saline.
<p>
The objective of this study was to demonstrate the utility of the Shuttle Emergency Eyewash
(SEE) system. The SEE is a device designed to rinse an individual's eyes with large volumes of
water in the event of exposure to toxic chemicals or irritants. It has been specially designed to
operate under zero gravity conditions without releasing water into the crew compartment.
<p>
Specific objectives of this test were:<br>
1. Evaluate the capability to flush the eyes with water for extended periods in microgravity
using the SEE system.<br>
2. Evaluate the SEE system on several crew members to assess function on anatomical
variations.<br>
3. Evaluate the SEE system for water leakage.<br>
4. Evaluate physical interfaces with galley auxiliary water port and Waste Collection System
(WCS) urinal hose.<br>
5. Evaluate the ability of the WCS to handle effluent water from the SEE.<br>
6. Evaluate the ability of the galley auxiliary water port to provide adequate potable water
flow rates.<br>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
For on-orbit use the SEE system was connected to the galley auxiliary water port via the
Personal Hygiene Hose (PHH) for water input and to the WCS urinal hose for water output. Alter
both connections were complete, the subject donned the SEE goggles. Another crewmember
was present to aid in the evaluation, if help was required, and to document all activity with the
camcorder. Alter all set-up activities were complete, the subject then activated the WCS airflow and initiated water flow with the PHH manual valve. The subject flushed the eyes for approximately two minutes, which was the required operating time. Once water flow was ceased, WCS airflow was de-activated and the goggles were removed. Residual water contained in the goggles was absorbed with a towel.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The SEE system evaluation was conducted on three subjects. The system was found to be
adequate to deliver continuous water flow to the eyes. The anatomical variations between each
subject did not create any major leakage problems with the system, although minor leaks were
reported. The leaks are believed to be created when water flow is activated. The initial backpressure from the trapped air in the goggles forces the goggles away from the face. This
transient response can be overcome by holding the goggles against the face until adequate water flow across the eyes is obtained. The connections to the PHH and the WCS were leak free and allowed appropriate water flow without incident.
<p>
